# Result

The ALU successfully performed all implemented arithmetic and logical operations.

The simulation confirmed that the operation control signal correctly selected the desired operation and that the resulting values matched the expected results.

The zero flag also correctly indicated when the ALU result was zero.

---

# Reflection

The ALU was the final project in the Computer Components section and brought together many of the concepts learned previously.

The earlier projects focused on individual building blocks such as registers, memory and communication interfaces. The ALU showed how several of these concepts fit into a CPU datapath.

One important realization was that the ALU itself does not need a clock. It is combinational logic, meaning that its output is determined directly by its inputs and operation signal.

The project also reinforced the difference between arithmetic operations and bitwise logical operations.

---

# Problems

The main challenge was making sure that each operation produced the correct result and that the operation control signal selected the correct logic.

Testing binary operations manually helped verify the expected results.

Another important part was testing edge cases such as zero results and bit shifting.

